WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:03.759
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.759 --> 00:00:08.280
Today we are going to open an AI-powered hair
salon.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.580
How cool is that.

00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:11.970
But, how?

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:17.490
Well, we recently looked at this related technique
from NVIDIA that is able to make our rough

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:21.589
drawings come to life as beautiful photorealistic
images.

00:00:21.589 --> 00:00:26.960
In goes a rough drawing, and out comes an
image of this quality.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.170
And it processes changes to the drawings with
the speed of thought.

00:00:31.170 --> 00:00:37.470
You can even request a bunch of variations
on the same theme, and get them right away.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:43.540
So here is a crazy idea - how about doing
this with hair styles?

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:49.400
Just draw them, and the AI puts it onto the
model and makes it look photorealistic.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:54.490
Well, that sounds like science fiction, so
I will believe it when I see it.

00:00:54.490 --> 00:01:00.720
And, this new method claims to be able to
do exactly that, so, let’s see the pros

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.910
first.

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:05.189
This will go in, and this will come out.

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:06.950
How is that even possible?

00:01:06.950 --> 00:01:13.720
Well, all we need to do is once again, produce
a crude drawing that is just enough to convey

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.100
our intentions.

00:01:15.100 --> 00:01:21.560
We can even control the color and the length
of the hair locks, or, even request a braid,

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:23.890
and all of these work pretty well.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:29.030
Another great aspect of this technique is
that it works very rapidly, so we can easily

00:01:29.030 --> 00:01:34.159
iterate over these hair styles, and if we
don’t feel like our original idea will be

00:01:34.159 --> 00:01:38.179
the one, we can refine it for as long as we
please.

00:01:38.179 --> 00:01:41.780
The AI too works with the speed of thought.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:43.959
One of the best aspects of this work.

00:01:43.959 --> 00:01:45.789
You can see an example here.

00:01:45.789 --> 00:01:51.351
This also does not look right…yet, but this
quick iteration also means that we can also

00:01:51.351 --> 00:01:53.939
lengthen those braids in just a moment.

00:01:53.939 --> 00:01:56.380
And, yes, better!

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:59.939
So, how does it perform compared to previous
techniques.

00:01:59.939 --> 00:02:02.069
Well, let’s have a look together!

00:02:02.069 --> 00:02:08.200
This is the input hairstyle, and a matte telling
the AI where we seek to place the hair, and,

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.500
let’s see the previous methods.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:17.230
The first two techniques are very rough, this
followup work is significantly better than

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:23.220
the previous ones, but it is still well behind
the true photograph for reference.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.980
This is much more realistic.

00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:30.420
So, hold on to your papers, and let’s see
the new technique.

00:02:30.420 --> 00:02:34.099
Now you’re probably thinking Károly…what’s
going on?

00:02:34.099 --> 00:02:36.599
Why is the video not changing?

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:37.750
Is this an error?

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:39.849
Well, I have to tell you something.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:41.120
It’s not an error.

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:44.560
What you are looking at now is not the reference
photo.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.590
No-no.

00:02:45.590 --> 00:02:48.470
This is the result of the new technique.

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:51.299
The true reference photo is this one.

00:02:51.299 --> 00:02:53.090
That is a wonderful result.

00:02:53.090 --> 00:02:59.690
The new method understands not only the geometry
of the hair better, but also how the hair

00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:02.940
should play with the illumination of the scene
too.

00:03:02.940 --> 00:03:08.310
I am a light transport researcher by trade,
and this makes me very, very happy.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:15.269
So much so, that in some ways, this technique
looks more realistic than the true image.

00:03:15.269 --> 00:03:17.260
That is super cool.

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:18.780
What a time to be alive!

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.569
Now, not even this technique is perfect.

00:03:21.569 --> 00:03:27.060
Even though the new method understands geometry
better than its predecessors, we have to be

00:03:27.060 --> 00:03:31.689
careful with our edits, because geometry problems
still emerge.

00:03:31.689 --> 00:03:32.689
Look here.

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:38.650
Also, the other issue is that the resolution
of the generated hair should match the resolution

00:03:38.650 --> 00:03:40.510
of the underlying image.

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.180
I feel that these is usually a bit more pixelated.

00:03:44.180 --> 00:03:49.300
Now note that these kinds of issues are what
typically get improved from one paper to the

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:54.629
next, so, you know the deal, a couple more
papers down the line, and I am sure, these

00:03:54.629 --> 00:03:58.079
edits will become nearly indistinguishable
from reality.

00:03:58.079 --> 00:04:04.489
As you see, there are still issues, the results
are often not even close to perfect, but,

00:04:04.489 --> 00:04:08.600
the pace of progress in AI research is nothing
short of amazing.

00:04:08.600 --> 00:04:16.139
With this, we can kind of open an AI-powered
hair salon, and some cases will work, but

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:21.930
soon, it might be that nearly all of our drawing
will result in photorealistic results that

00:04:21.930 --> 00:05:09.970
are indistinguishable from reality.

00:05:09.970 --> 00:05:12.890
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

